<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chenani-Nashri Tunnel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Dr. Syama Prasad Mookerjee Tunnel, Patnitop Tunnel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chenani-Nashri Tunnel is a road tunnel of about 9.2 kilometres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> highway in Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, running beneath the Patnitop range between Chenani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udhampur">Udhampur</a> district and Nashri in Ramban district. Opened in April 2017, it was then India's longest road tunnel and shortened the route by around 30 kilometres, bypassing a stretch often closed by snow and landslides. It was later renamed after Syama Prasad Mookerjee.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chenani-nashri-tunnel.jpg" alt="Chenani-Nashri Tunnel" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sidheeq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chenani-Nashri_Highway_Tunnel_Night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tunnel was built to bypass an old section of the highway full of hairpin bends, with places such as Patnitop, Kud and Batote prone to snow and avalanches, which blocked the road every winter and left long queues of vehicles, sometimes for days. The foundation was laid in July 2011 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and Kashmir Chief Minister Omar Abdullah with the Union Health Minister Ghulam Nabi Azad, under the UPA government. The project then stalled for some years amid funding difficulties, and its cost rose from an estimated ₹2,520 crore to ₹3,720 crore.</p><p>Completion was planned for May 2016 and then July 2016, but a dispute between the workers and the executing agency held up the work. Snowfall in early 2017 cut the electricity needed for testing, and the independent engineers reported that the demonetisation of 8 November 2016 had left contractors unable to pay their labourers. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narendra-modi">Narendra Modi</a> inaugurated the tunnel on 2 April 2017. Nearly 1,500 engineers, geologists and labourers worked on it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tunnel carries two lanes of National Highway 44 at an altitude of about 1,200 metres in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>, from about 2 kilometres from Chenani, south of Patnitop, to Nashri village north of it. It has two parallel tubes, a main traffic tube 13 metres across and an escape tube 6 metres across, joined by 29 cross passages every 300 metres. Fresh air enters the main tube every 8 metres and is drawn out into the escape tube every 100 metres, making it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first road tunnel with transverse ventilation and the country's first with a fully integrated tunnel control system.</p><p>A control room outside the tunnel watches 124 cameras and a linear heat detection system. SOS boxes stand every 150 metres, the lighting is graded to ease the change of light at the portals, and in a fire the supply of fresh air stops while fans draw the smoke away and emergency vehicles use the escape tube. The tunnel bypasses 44 avalanche and landslide-prone spots and cuts travel time between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> by two hours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dr. Syama Prasad Mookerjee Tunnel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chenani is now a town in Udhampur district, known for the <b>Chenani-Nashri</b> road tunnel.</blockquote><a href="/state/chenani-state">Chenani State</a></li></ul></section>
